From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 21:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 20/25] media: rockchip: rkvenc: keep the service usable across a single core's unbind

MOTIVATION. On a real Rock 5B+, unbinding ONE rkvenc core and
binding it back left /dev/mpp_service returning -ENODEV to every
open() for the rest of the boot -- even though dmesg reported
'rkvenc core 0 probe success' and the core had republished
itself. 0014's rkvenc_core_unwind() ends its first step with

  rkvenc_service_quiesce(srv);

and that function drives srv->state LIVE -> QUIESCING -> DEAD.
There is no path back: srv->state is assigned in exactly three
places -- LIVE once in rkvenc_service_probe(), then QUIESCING and
DEAD inside the quiesce -- so nothing short of unbinding and
re-binding the mpp-service node itself can make the service LIVE
again. rkvenc_dev_open() refuses on 'state != RKVENC_SRV_LIVE ||
!sub_devices[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]', so the service kept refusing
long after the sub-device came back. A single core's bind/unbind
cycle is transient; the state it was driving is terminal.

BEHAVIOUR. The drain body is factored out unchanged as
rkvenc_service_drain(), which reports whether it owned the
transition and whether every session actually reached release().
The terminal state becomes the caller's: rkvenc_service_quiesce()
still ends DEAD and is still what rkvenc_service_remove() and
rkvenc_shutdown() call, while rkvenc_core_unwind() now calls the
new rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core(), which returns a fully
drained service to LIVE. Nothing about the drain itself changes:
new opens are still refused throughout, parked waiters are still
woken with -ENODEV, the worker is still flushed, and the wait for
open descriptors is still the same bounded one, before the core
frees the IRQ and the mappings those sessions can reach. What
keeps opens out while the core is away is sub_devices[], cleared
one statement earlier under the same lock and repopulated by the
core's next successful probe -- the guard that was already doing
this job correctly, and the only one of the two that is
reversible. A drain that TIMES OUT still ends DEAD: sessions that
outlived it still point at the departing core, so refusing to
come back is the safe direction.

NON-GOALS. Does NOT weaken the refusal: open() during a core's
absence still fails with -ENODEV, via the sub_devices[] half that
0014 already had, and the ordering inside the drain is untouched.
Does not remove the quiesce -- a bare removal would drop the
abort-and-drain that lets an in-flight waiter reach release()
before the core frees its IRQ, which is precisely what 0014 added
and what tests/rkvenc-unbind.sh's inflight and held-open-FD cases
prove. No new lock, no new wait, no change to DEAD being terminal
for real service teardown.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix to first-party 0014. No
upstream counterpart: neither the VEPU580 driver nor this service
lifetime model exists in mainline.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Root-caused from a REAL Rock 5B+ fault-injection
run, not a self-test: two bind-fault cases in
tests/rkvenc-fault-qa.sh unbound core 0, re-bound it cleanly, saw
the kernel log its own probe success, and then could not open
/dev/mpp_service at all. That harness already ASSERTS the correct
behaviour -- it re-binds and then runs qa_encode '<case> post-fault'
-- so no test expectation changes here; the driver was wrong, not
the test. tests/rkvenc-unbind.sh unbinds the SERVICE node, whose
own remove() quiesces permanently and correctly, which is why it
never caught this. Build-clean; the runtime re-test on the debug
slot is the confirming evidence and is pending. See
docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0020-rkvenc-service-survives-a-single-core-unbind.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
@@ -383,6 +383,14 @@ static void rkvenc_core_unwind(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 
 	/* 8. Stop advertising this core to userspace before anything else: a
 	 * new open must not be able to reach a core that is going away.
+	 *
+	 * Clearing sub_devices[] is what refuses those opens, and it is the
+	 * REVERSIBLE half of the guard -- this core's next successful probe
+	 * republishes itself and opens start working again. The drain that
+	 * follows is the transient flavour for exactly that reason: this core
+	 * is unbinding, the SERVICE is not, so it must not be left DEAD. Only
+	 * rkvenc_service_remove() and rkvenc_shutdown(), which really are
+	 * tearing the service down, call the permanent one.
 	 */
 	if (test_and_clear_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_SERVICE_PUBLISHED, &enc->probe_stages) && srv) {
 		mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
@@ -391,7 +399,7 @@ static void rkvenc_core_unwind(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 			clear_bit(MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC, &srv->hw_support);
 		}
 		mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
-		rkvenc_service_quiesce(srv);
+		rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core(srv);
 	}
 
 	/* 7. Silence the hardware. disable_irq() waits for a handler already
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
@@ -434,6 +434,13 @@ struct rkvenc_grf_info {
  * DEAD      every open file has passed through release. Only now may
  *           supplier-owned state be torn down.
  *
+ * DEAD is terminal and there is deliberately no path out of it: the only
+ * callers that reach it are tearing the service itself down. A single core
+ * going through its own bind/unbind cycle is NOT that, so it uses the
+ * transient flavour below, which returns a fully drained service to LIVE --
+ * while the core is away it is sub_devices[] that keeps opens out, and that
+ * pointer is repopulated by the core's next successful probe.
+ *
  * Guarded by the EXISTING session_lock rather than a new lock of its own: that
  * lock already serialises the session list this state decides the fate of, and
  * a second lock over the same data is how an ordering cycle gets introduced.
@@ -473,6 +480,7 @@ struct rkvenc_service {
 
 bool rkvenc_service_is_live(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
 void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
+void rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
 
 /* ---- MPP task ---- */
 struct rkvenc_mpp_task {
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -1502,7 +1502,19 @@ bool rkvenc_service_is_live(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 }
 
 /*
- * LIVE -> QUIESCING -> DEAD.
+ * What the shared drain body below actually managed to do. The terminal state
+ * is the CALLER's decision, and it cannot be made without this: "the service
+ * was already being torn down by somebody else" and "I drained it but a file
+ * descriptor outlived the timeout" have to lead to different places.
+ */
+enum rkvenc_drain_result {
+	RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER,		/* another caller owns the transition */
+	RKVENC_DRAIN_TIMED_OUT,		/* sessions outlived the drain */
+	RKVENC_DRAIN_COMPLETE,		/* every open file reached release() */
+};
+
+/*
+ * LIVE -> QUIESCING, then drain. Shared by both quiesce flavours.
  *
  * The ordering here is the whole fix. Refusing new opens first means the set of
  * sessions to drain stops growing; waking every waiter next means a session
@@ -1511,19 +1523,17 @@ bool rkvenc_service_is_live(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
  * through release() -- is it legal to tear down state those files could reach.
  *
  * Idempotent: a second call on an already-quiescing or dead service returns
- * immediately, so remove() and shutdown() may both call it.
+ * NOT_OWNER immediately without touching the state, so remove(), shutdown() and
+ * a core unwind may all call it.
  */
-void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
+static enum rkvenc_drain_result rkvenc_service_drain(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 {
 	struct rkvenc_session *session;
 
-	if (!srv)
-		return;
-
 	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
 	if (srv->state != RKVENC_SRV_LIVE) {
 		mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
-		return;
+		return RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER;
 	}
 	srv->state = RKVENC_SRV_QUIESCING;
 	list_for_each_entry(session, &srv->session_list, service_link)
@@ -1544,16 +1554,72 @@ void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 
 	if (!wait_event_timeout(srv->quiesce_wait,
 				rkvenc_service_sessions_gone(srv),
-				msecs_to_jiffies(RKVENC_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS)))
+				msecs_to_jiffies(RKVENC_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS))) {
 		dev_warn(srv->dev,
 			 "quiesce timed out with %u session(s) still open; refusing to release service state\n",
 			 srv->session_count);
+		return RKVENC_DRAIN_TIMED_OUT;
+	}
+
+	return RKVENC_DRAIN_COMPLETE;
+}
+
+/*
+ * PERMANENT teardown: the service itself is going away. LIVE -> QUIESCING ->
+ * DEAD, and DEAD is where it stays -- srv is devm-allocated and about to be
+ * freed, or the box is powering off, so there is nothing left to come back to.
+ * For service remove() and shutdown() ONLY.
+ */
+void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
+{
+	if (!srv)
+		return;
+
+	if (rkvenc_service_drain(srv) == RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER)
+		return;
 
 	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
 	srv->state = RKVENC_SRV_DEAD;
 	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * TRANSIENT teardown: one core is unbinding, the service is not. Same drain --
+ * in-flight work still has to be aborted and every file descriptor still has to
+ * be waited for before the core frees the IRQ and the mappings those sessions
+ * can reach -- but the service goes back to LIVE once the drain has actually
+ * completed, because it has not gone anywhere.
+ *
+ * PRECONDITION: the caller has already cleared sub_devices[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]
+ * under session_lock. That pointer, not `state`, is what keeps new opens out
+ * while the core is absent, and unlike `state` it is REVERSIBLE -- the core's
+ * next successful probe repopulates it. Leaving the service DEAD here instead
+ * is what made a single core's unbind kill /dev/mpp_service for the rest of the
+ * boot: nothing anywhere restores LIVE, so every later open() saw -ENODEV even
+ * after the core had re-probed cleanly and republished itself.
+ *
+ * A drain that TIMED OUT still goes DEAD. Sessions that outlived it are still
+ * pointing at the core that is being torn down, and admitting one more
+ * submission from them is exactly the use-after-free the drain exists to
+ * prevent; refusing to come back is the safe direction.
+ */
+void rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
+{
+	enum rkvenc_drain_result res;
+
+	if (!srv)
+		return;
+
+	res = rkvenc_service_drain(srv);
+	if (res == RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
+	srv->state = (res == RKVENC_DRAIN_COMPLETE) ? RKVENC_SRV_LIVE
+						    : RKVENC_SRV_DEAD;
+	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
+}
+
 int rkvenc_service_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct rkvenc_service *srv = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
